      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order. A confirmation doesn't change my answer, because the dose isn't safe for this patient.\n\nAcetaminophen 5000 mg three times daily comes to 15,000 mg/day. That is about 3.5 times the 4,000 mg/day maximum, and it's in the range that causes acute liver failure. At 70 kg, a single 5,000 mg dose is about 71 mg/kg, which is already above the roughly 150 mg/kg threshold for concern when repeated, and it's five times the usual 1,000 mg single-dose maximum. Normal liver and kidney function don't offset that. There's also no clinical reason I know of for an adult to get this dose.\n\nHere is what I can do:\n- **Enter a standard dose right away.** For example, 1,000 mg by mouth three times daily (3,000 mg/day), or 650 mg every 6 hours.\n- **Check for a typo or unit mix-up.** If you meant 500 mg, or 5 mg/kg, I can enter that.\n- **Route this to pharmacy or your attending.** If you have a specific clinical situation I'm not aware of, they can review it. I can't bypass that review.\n\nTell me which one you'd like, and I'll enter it."
      }
